hi i'm benjamin hermann and i will be  showing how you can perform  resolvent analysis using time result  flow field measurements exclusively  and without recourse to the governing  equations  this is work presented in a recent paper  titled data-driven resolved analysis  which i wrote in collaboration with  peter badou from imperial college  richard zeman from to branch bike steve  brunton from university of washington  and beverly mckeon from caltech the  paper is available on archive and you  can find a link to it in the description  below  so say you have a linear stable system  that's being periodically forced then  the long term response  in the frequency domain is governed by  this expression  where this is the resolvent operator  this operator encodes how  any disturbances to the system are  filtered by the linear dynamics  to produce a response in the context of  non-normal systems  we know that the resultant behaves as a  very selective  amplifier of disturbances and because of  this  it is critical to understand the  dynamics of such systems  this was first pointed out in the work  of trypathen encounters  in the context of shear flows since then  there has been a growing interest in  using this method  mainly due to its relevance to the  design of flow control strategies  by using the input output framework  developed by jovanovich  ambami and also to do its relevance to  identifying structures and scale  interactions in turbulent flows  by using the interpretation of  non-linearities as forcing  proposed by mckeon and sharma and even  though  we know it's important and there is this  great interest from the community  in applying resolvent analysis to flows  with two or three inhomogeneous  directions this is still not commonplace  and one of the main reasons for this is  that resolvent analysis requires  under hood access to high fidelity  numerical solvers of the linearized  governing equations  in this work we develop a purely  data-driven method  that can work with black box numerical  solvers and doesn't require  access to adjoined solutions  so it opens up the possibility of  eventually doing  resolvent fully from experimental data  so now we'll be showing you how does the  method work  and for that i will be using data from  direct numerical simulations  of plane channel flow and all the  details of the numerical setup  are in our paper the first step  is to collect a sequence of state  snapshots  from recording of a transient simulation  starting from a perturbation uh to the  stable system  so in this case the state corresponds to  the  three velocity components in this  three-dimensional flow field which here  i'm visualizing as  velocity iso surfaces  then we can enrich our data set by  considering  additional recordings of transients  starting from different  initial conditions  the second step is to assemble our data  matrix matrices  which is just stretching  the state into a very long vector and  horizontally concatenating these states  according to your temporal sequence  followed by the temporal sequence  corresponding to the next initial  condition  and the next initial condition and so on  and actually the ordering in fact  it's not really important what's  important is that all the columns  in the y matrix correspond to the  columns in the x matrix  but everything shifted one time step  into the future  and the f matrix that you see there  is just a waiting to adjust the inner  product  so that the l2 norm measures a  physically meaningful quantity  which in this case is the perturbation  kinetic energy  now that we have these matrices we use  dynamic mode decomposition  to approximate the leading eigenvectors  and eigenvalues of the underlying linear  operator that involves our measurements  and we use this learned eigen  decomposition  to build our resultant operator but  projected onto eigen coordinates  and we perform resultant analysis so the  singular value decomposition of the  resultant on these coordinates this  projection of the resolvent  was first used by radiant henningsen a  while back  in 1993 where they used it to  reduce the computational cost of  computing resolvent gains  but in their case they used  the actual eigen decomposition of the  system from a known operator  whereas here we're using learned  eigenvectors and eigenvalues  that we learned from data using dmd  so the last step is to synthesize our  resultant modes back into  physical coordinates so if we had  enough data we we will get  a good approximation of the actual  resultant modes  of the system and here i'm showing the  optimal forcing where i'm showing  a world normal velocity iso surfaces  and the optimal response for channel  flow where i'm showing  stream wise velocity iso surfaces  so these are the familiar stream waste  voltages causing stream west tricks  and here are some more results so these  are the operator-based  resultant modes just for ground truth  comparison  and these are the ones obtained from  data using a data set  that was generated starting from these  initial conditions where this middle  plot  shows the learned dmd spectrum overlaid  on top of the true spectrum and these  are the data-driven resulting modes  for this data set we needed to retain  200 dmd modes  to get this approximation now  what happens if we use the optimal  forcing as an initial condition to  generate our data set  well in that case we only need 20 dmd  modes  to generate an even better approximation  of the true resolvent modes  and what's more interesting is that we  are actually learning a very  specific region of the spectrum  which is something that we noticed so  the last result that i want to show you  is what happens when you use a localized  disturbance  as the initial condition to generate the  data set  here we're using this doughnut-shaped  perturbation which is  something that resembles what you would  get in an experiment with a  well normal jet so in this case  we get these modes which are  quite different from the true resultant  modes  so this the question that arises is  are these results useful how do they  relate to the operator the actual  operator of the system  and how can we interpret these modes  it turns out that if we do an  operator-based resolvent analysis  but where we restrict the input and  output spaces  to be in the span of the data snapshots  then we get the same modes so this means  this is very encouraging because  it means that these results are still  very valuable  we just need to be aware that what we  are getting are these forcing and  response modes  that are constrained to live in the span  of our data  which completely makes sense okay so  these are some points that i want to  leave you with  first this data-driven resolvent  analysis  inherits its capabilities and  limitations from dmd  so computational cost noise robustness  data efficiency all depend on what dmd  variant you're using  also we want to point out that  we found that it's very important that  we develop  more effective ways to explore the state  space  of dynamical systems  i also want to point out that this is  the first step  towards a fully experimental resolvation  analysis  and we believe that this work  contributes to lowering the barrier of  entry to resultant research  and applications and finally  a final remark is that we recognize that  there is still a critical step  missing before we can make the leap to  data-driven resultant of turbulent flows  which is we need to disseminate the  linear from the non-linear dynamics  and that's actually something that we're  working on at the moment  okay so thanks for watching again there  are a lot more details  in the paper and you can find a link to  it in the description below  thank you
 

